Wave 5 builds a formal reasoning, logic, and probability layer over prior [[Inference]], [[Reasoning]], epistemology, semantics, and scientific-method concepts.

== Dependency order ==

<pre>
Logic
 ├→ Argument
 │    ├→ Deductive reasoning
 │    ├→ Inductive reasoning
 │    └→ Abductive reasoning
 ├→ Contradiction
 ├→ Consistency (logic)
 ├→ Propositional logic
 ├→ First-order logic
 └→ Modal logic

Mathematics
 ├→ Proof
 └→ Conjecture

Probability
 ├→ Conditional probability
 ├→ Probability distribution
 └→ Bayesian inference

Correlation
 └→ statistical association

Belief revision
 └→ full-belief change under consistency/rationality constraints
</pre>

== Normalized definitions ==

=== Logic ===
Logic studies systems of consequence, validity, inference, formal language, proof, and semantics.

=== Deductive reasoning ===
Deductive reasoning aims at conclusions that are guaranteed by the premises under a specified consequence relation.

=== Inductive reasoning ===
Inductive reasoning is ampliative reasoning in which premises support but do not deductively entail a conclusion.

=== Abductive reasoning ===
Abductive reasoning generates or supports hypotheses partly because they would explain the observed facts, often under an inference-to-the-best-explanation interpretation.

=== Argument ===
An argument is a structured set of claims in which premises are offered as reasons for a conclusion.

=== Proof ===
A mathematical proof is a rigorous demonstration that a statement follows from accepted assumptions, definitions, prior results, and inference rules.

=== Conjecture ===
A conjecture is a mathematical statement proposed as true but not yet proved or disproved.

=== Contradiction ===
A contradiction is a logical conflict such as a proposition together with its negation, with exact consequences depending on the logic.

=== Consistency (logic) ===
Logical consistency is the absence of derivable contradiction or triviality under a specified formal system.

=== Probability ===
Probability is a mathematical framework for quantifying chance, uncertainty, or degrees of support under specified axioms and interpretations.

=== Probability distribution ===
A probability distribution assigns probabilities to values or events associated with a random variable.

=== Correlation ===
Correlation is statistical association between variables; Pearson correlation is one linear measure among many.

=== Conditional probability ===
Conditional probability P(A|B) is the probability assigned to A when B is taken as given.

=== Bayesian inference ===
Bayesian inference updates probabilistic beliefs or parameter/model uncertainty by combining prior distributions with likelihoods to obtain posterior distributions.

=== Belief revision ===
Belief revision studies rational changes to bodies of full beliefs when information is added, removed, or conflicts with existing commitments.

=== Propositional logic ===
Propositional logic studies formulas built from whole-sentence variables and propositional connectives.

=== First-order logic ===
First-order logic extends propositional structure with predicates, terms, variables, and quantification over individuals.

=== Modal logic ===
Modal logic extends logical languages with operators for modes such as necessity, possibility, knowledge, belief, time, or obligation.

