== Reasoning boundary ==

Wave 4 [[Reasoning]] is the broad cognitive umbrella.

Wave 5 descendants are formal/general reasoning modes:

<pre>
Reasoning
 ├→ Deductive reasoning
 ├→ Inductive reasoning
 └→ Abductive reasoning
</pre>

These pages must not rewrite the cognitive-science definition of reasoning.

== Logic boundary ==

Classical logic is central but not exhaustive.

The Core/Technical surfaces should expose the distinction:

<pre>
formal language
   + proof system
   + semantics
   → consequence relation
</pre>

Soundness and completeness relate derivability and semantic validity but are properties/theorems of systems, not automatic facts for every logic.

== Probability boundary ==

The mathematical calculus and the philosophical interpretation of probability remain distinct.

A standard axiomatic presentation may use:

<pre>
P(A) ≥ 0
P(Ω) = 1
P(A ∪ B) = P(A)+P(B) for disjoint A,B
</pre>

without committing the encyclopedia to one interpretation of what probabilities mean.

== Bayesian boundary ==

Bayesian inference uses Bayes' rule inside a broader modeling workflow:

<pre>
prior × likelihood
        ↓
posterior
        ↓
prediction / estimation / comparison / decision
</pre>

Bayes' theorem is an identity; Bayesian inference additionally requires a model, prior, likelihood, conditioning assumptions, and interpretation.

== Belief-revision boundary ==

AGM is the canonical historical framework but not the whole field.

Core/Technical should distinguish:

* expansion;
* contraction;
* revision;
* belief sets versus belief bases;
* prioritized versus non-prioritized change;
* full beliefs versus probabilistic credences.

== Formal-logic boundary ==

[[Propositional logic]] handles sentence-level connectives.

[[First-order logic]] adds predicates and quantification over individuals.

[[Modal logic]] adds operators interpreted over modal structure such as possible worlds/states.

These systems can be combined; they remain distinct canonical concepts.

== Public concept versus AI/BITwiki application ==

<pre>
argument           ≠ hidden chain-of-thought
proof              ≠ software test result by definition
probability        ≠ model confidence score by default
Bayesian inference ≠ any iterative update
belief revision    ≠ agent memory mutation
first-order logic  ≠ ontology schema
modal logic        ≠ workflow state machine
</pre>

Applications may appear only after the public concept is established.

